<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		UIBackgroundModes, remote-notification. WITHOUT THIS KEY IOS DOES NOT
		WAKE THE APPLICATION FOR A PUSH and the Capacitor plugin never sees
		one. It is the plist half of push; the entitlement in
		`App.entitlements` is the other half, and BOTH are needed.

		`remote-notification` alone does NOT make this a background app for
		App Review purposes: it is what every application that receives a
		notification declares. What it does not permit is a silent push used
		to keep a process alive, and nothing in this product sends one. Every
		push this platform sends is an alert with a title and a body a person
		will see, which is also what `userVisibleOnly` promises on the web
		side in `components/app/push/enrol.ts`.

		`audio` (VC1, 8 October 2026): a voice or video call the member is
		in keeps its sound when they lock the screen or switch apps, as on
		every calling app. It is used only while a call is live; nothing
		plays or records in the background otherwise.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>
  <key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
        <string>Vallo</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<!--
		Both version keys are build settings rather than literals, and they stay
		that way. `MARKETING_VERSION` and `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` live in
		`App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj`, and `scripts/sync-native-versions.mjs`
		writes them there from `apps/web/package.json` in the same run that
		writes the Android pair. Putting a literal here would give the platform a
		fourth place to hold a version number and a fourth place for it to drift.

		What each one means to App Store Connect, because they are easy to swap:
		`CFBundleShortVersionString` is what a person sees on the store page, and
		`CFBundleVersion` is the build number, which must increase with every
		upload inside a given marketing version and is never shown to anyone.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<!--
		Export compliance, answered once here instead of on every upload.

		Without this key App Store Connect asks the encryption question against
		each build and holds the build out of TestFlight until somebody answers
		it. The honest answer is that this application uses only https and the
		platform's own keychain and cookie storage, which is the exemption
		Apple's own documentation describes, so nothing here is subject to
		export regulations. If a future release ever adds its own cryptography,
		this is a legal declaration and it has to be revisited rather than left.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
		App Transport Security, written out although it matches the default.

		The default already refuses plain http, and that is the behaviour we
		want: the shell loads the live origin, the web platform sends HSTS with
		preload, `capacitor.config.ts` sets `cleartext: false` and the Android
		manifest sets `usesCleartextTraffic="false"`. This key is here so that
		the next person who hits a local development host over http adds an
		exception deliberately, with this comment in front of them, rather than
		reaching for `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` and shipping it.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<!--
		PURPOSE STRINGS.

		Apple shows these words verbatim in the permission alert, and App Review
		rejects a string that restates the permission instead of explaining what
		the person gets for granting it. So each one below names the feature, in
		the second person, and says where the data goes. They are also the only
		place on the platform where the honest answer "it never leaves your
		device" can be said to somebody at the moment they are deciding.

		The inventory in `docs/MOBILE_READINESS.md` section 4 is the source of
		truth for which keys exist here at all. Every key present is a
		capability something in `src/` actually reaches for.
	-->
	<!--
		THIS COMMENT AND THE STRING UNDER IT WERE BOTH FALSE, AND THE STRING IS
		THE KIND OF FALSE THAT IS A 5.1.1(ii) AND 5.1.5 REFUSAL.

		It said, in the alert a person reads before they decide: "Your position
		is used on this device only and is never sent to Vallo." This comment
		said MapCanvas was the only consumer. Neither was true. Two other
		surfaces took a position and sent it to our own origin, one of them
		storing a six decimal fix on a database row. Apple 5.1.1(ii) is one
		sentence, "Ensure your purpose strings clearly and completely describe
		your use of the data", and a string that promises the opposite of what
		the code does is the clearest way there is to fail it.

		WHICH OF THE TWO ANSWERS THIS IS. The instruction was: stop sending it
		or tell the truth, and say which and why. Both, in fact, and split by
		whether the surface was a real capability or a broken one.

		  - `TravelTime` was DELETED. It posted a precise fix to
		    `/api/travel-time`, a route that has never existed, and its designed
		    failure was to hide itself. It took a permission and gave nothing
		    back. There was nothing there to describe honestly.

		  - `HostWizard` STAYS and the string now describes it. A host standing
		    in the property they are listing taps a control to drop the pin, and
		    the coordinate becomes THE PROPERTY'S address on the listing. That
		    is not us following a person around; it is the one datum the listing
		    is about. Removing a real capability to make a false sentence true
		    would have been the worse trade.

		THE TWO CONSUMERS, WHICH IS NOW THE WHOLE LIST:
		  1. `src/components/app/search/MapCanvas.tsx:489` recentres the map and
		     posts nothing. This one always matched the old promise.
		  2. `src/components/host/HostWizard.tsx:616` reads a fix, rounds it to
		     six decimals and saves it through `addAccommodationDraft` as the
		     latitude and longitude of the property being listed.

		`WhenInUse` with deliberately no `Always` variant: nothing runs in the
		background, and asking for background location would be a review
		question with no answer.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vallo uses your location in two places, and only when you tap for it. Tapping locate on the search map centres it on where you are, and that stays on this device. Tapping use my location while you are listing a property saves those coordinates as the address of that property, so they are sent to Vallo and kept on the listing.</string>
	<!--
		Read when the person picks an existing image or document from the file
		inputs behind listing photos, profile pictures, message attachments and
		agent verification uploads. Nothing on this platform writes to the photo
		library, so `NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription` is absent.
	-->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vallo opens your photos when you choose one to upload, so you can add pictures to a listing, set your profile picture, send a photo in a message, or supply an agent verification document. Only the files you pick are read.</string>
	<!--
		The camera, and why it is present when the permission inventory calls it
		unused.

		CORRECTED 8 OCTOBER 2026 (VC1): the paragraph below was true until calls.
		A call screen now opens a camera and microphone STREAM, and the site
		sends `camera=(self), microphone=(self)`. The note at the two strings
		further down says what changed.

		Both statements were true, because they are about two different
		mechanisms. `docs/MOBILE_READINESS.md` recorded that nothing on this
		platform opened a camera STREAM: no `getUserMedia` call existed in
		`src`, and the web platform sent `Permissions-Policy: camera=()` on
		every response to keep it that way.

		This key is not about that. Eleven `<input type="file">` elements exist,
		and the three behind listing photos, message attachments and agent
		verification carry `accept="image/*"`. On iOS a WKWebView renders that as
		an action sheet offering "Photo Library", "Take Photo or Video" and
		"Browse". The camera entry is drawn by the operating system's own picker
		and cannot be removed by the page, `Permissions-Policy` does not reach it
		because no web API is being called, and iOS TERMINATES an application
		that reaches the camera with no purpose string.

		So the choice was between a purpose string and a crash on one tap, in the
		middle of an agent uploading the document that gets them verified. The
		string wins, and it does not widen anything the web policy was holding
		shut: on iOS Safari the same input offers the same camera today, with
		Safari holding the permission instead of us.
	-->
	<!--
		STORE-P2-03. The listing walkthrough upload (`VideoWalkthrough.tsx`)
		accepts video, and on iOS its picker offers "Take Video". So this
		string no longer says Vallo never records video (that was false the
		moment the option existed), and the microphone string below exists:
		recording video with sound without one terminates the app.

		VC1 (8 October 2026): VOICE AND VIDEO CALLS. A call screen now asks for
		a camera and microphone STREAM through getUserMedia, inside the web
		view, and the site allows it for its own origin
		(`Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self)`). Capacitor's
		`WebViewDelegationHandler` answers WebKit's media-capture question for
		the page (iOS 15+, the deployment target here), and iOS then shows the
		system prompt with these strings. So both strings now name calls, and
		say what is true: nothing is recorded or kept, and a call uses the
		camera and microphone only while it is on screen. CallKit, background
		ringing and lock-screen answering are NOT built (no `voip` background
		mode, no PushKit); see docs/video-calling/VIDEO-CALLING-MOBILE-COMPATIBILITY.md.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vallo uses your camera when you start or answer a video call, and when you choose to take a photo or record a video: a place you are listing, a walkthrough video of it, a document that verifies you as an agent, or something you want to send in a message. Calls are not recorded, nothing is captured until you tap the shutter, and Vallo never opens the camera on its own.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vallo uses the microphone during a voice or video call you start or answer, and while you record a walkthrough video of a property you are listing so the video has sound. Calls are not recorded, and nothing is recorded until you start recording.</string>
	<!--
		FACE ID (7 October 2026). Vallo unlocks with Face ID through a WebAuthn
		platform key (passkey) in the web view, verified on the server; see the
		webcredentials note in App.entitlements. The system shows this string
		the first time Face ID is asked for on Vallo's behalf. Nothing is
		unlocked by the device alone: the server checks the key's signature
		before it lets anybody in.
	-->
	<key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Vallo uses Face ID so you can unlock the app and confirm payments with a look instead of typing your passcode. Your face never leaves this device, and your passcode always works too.</string>
	<!--
		DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and each one is a decision rather than an omission.

		NSContactsUsageDescription, NSCalendarsUsageDescription. Nothing reads
		an address book or writes a calendar entry, and asking would be a review
		question with no good answer.

		NSUserTrackingUsageDescription. There is no analytics vendor and no
		advertising identifier anywhere in this codebase (crash reports, when
		Sentry is configured, are not tracking), so there is no tracking to
		ask permission for. That is worth keeping true: it makes the App
		Privacy questionnaire short and honest.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
    <false/>
    <key>UISceneConfigurations</key>
    <dict>
      <key>UIWindowSceneSessionRoleApplication</key>
      <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UISceneConfigurationName</key>
            <string>Default Configuration</string>
            <key>UISceneDelegateClassName</key>
            <string>$(PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME).SceneDelegate</string>
            <key>UISceneStoryboardFile</key>
            <string>Main</string>
        </dict>
      </array>
    </dict>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<!--
		This said `armv7`, which the Capacitor template has carried for years
		and which is now wrong in a way that only shows up at submission. The
		deployment target is iOS 15 and no 32-bit device ever ran it, so the
		requirement described a set of devices that cannot install this
		application at all. `arm64` is the truthful statement and it is what
		App Store Connect expects to see beside a modern deployment target.
	-->
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<!-- STORE-13: iPhone only for v1 (TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY = 1), so there is
	     no iPad orientation list. iPads run it in iPhone compatibility mode. -->
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
